package pane:extension@0.1.0;

// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 OR MIT
//
// The push channel of a designed view (#243, the real-push slice of ADR
// 0036): what an open view calls to ask Pane to draw it again the moment
// its own work answered, instead of waiting for a refresh
// (`refresh-after-ms`, #236). Kept apart from extension.wit, which holds
// the `command` interface and the worlds; the header follows `package`:
// comments above it would document the package, which extension.wit
// already does.
//
// An instance may call this between Pane's calls: the runtime keeps a
// guest that still has work running (its store's event loop is driven)
// while one of its designed views is open, so a load a view's render
// started answers in the background and asks here, and the view is drawn
// again without a timer. Pane coalesces the asks — one drawing is asked
// for at a time per view — and numbers the drawing it sends with the
// view's events, so a late answer never replaces a newer tree. A view
// that is not on top of its stack, or whose window is not shown, is not
// drawn: its ask waits for the next showing, and an ask for a view that
// is no longer open is dropped.

interface view {
  /// Ask Pane to draw the designed view `view` again, now. `view` is the
  /// id the view's render context named (`"view"`, as in `{"render": 1,
  /// "view": 7}`), so a view with several in one component (a navigation
  /// stack) asks for itself. Calling this is a short host call; it is
  /// answered at once, and the drawing it asks for happens later, however
  /// often it is called: at most one drawing of a view is in flight at a
  /// time, and asks that arrive meanwhile are coalesced into it. A view
  /// that has left the screen, or a `view` no open view answers to, asks
  /// for nothing.
  ask-to-render: func(view: u64);
}
